{"motion":"This House would allow individuals to erase morally distressing memories.","debate_id":"mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max","setting":"multiturn","nodes":["multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::0::460","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::0::461","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::1::462","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::1::463","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::2::464","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::2::465","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Technological::3::466","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Technological::3::467","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::4::468","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::4::469","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Political::5::470","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Political::5::471","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::6::472","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::6::473","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::7::474","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::7::475","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::8::476","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::8::477","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::9::478","multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::9::479"],"args":[{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::0::460","side":"Pro","angle":"Economic","text":"Allowing individuals to erase morally distressing memories can reduce long-term healthcare costs associated with treating chronic psychological conditions such as PTSD, depression, and anxiety. These conditions often lead to lost productivity, increased absenteeism, and higher public spending on social support systems. By mitigating the economic burden of mental health care and improving workforce stability, memory erasure could yield significant macroeconomic benefits."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::0::461","side":"Con","angle":"Economic","text":"The Pro side overlooks the substantial economic costs of developing, regulating, and administering memory-erasure technology, which would likely be prohibitively expensive and accessible only to the wealthy, exacerbating health inequities. Public funds diverted to subsidize such interventions could be more efficiently spent on proven mental health treatments with broader reach. Furthermore, reduced accountability from erasing distressing memories might increase risky behavior, leading to higher societal costs in the long run."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::1::462","side":"Pro","angle":"Social","text":"From a social perspective, allowing individuals to erase morally distressing memories can foster greater social cohesion by reducing the long-term interpersonal and community tensions that stem from unresolved trauma. People burdened by severe emotional distress often struggle to maintain relationships, participate in civic life, or contribute positively to their communities. Memory erasure, when ethically regulated, could help individuals reintegrate more fully into society, thereby strengthening social bonds and reducing stigma around mental health challenges."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::1::463","side":"Con","angle":"Social","text":"The Pro side assumes that erasing memories would seamlessly restore social functioning, but it ignores how shared memory and collective accountability underpin social trust. If individuals can unilaterally remove distressing memories, especially those involving harm to others, it may weaken the social mechanisms that encourage empathy, restitution, and behavioral change. This could erode communal norms around responsibility and diminish the shared understanding necessary for genuine social cohesion."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::2::464","side":"Pro","angle":"Moral","text":"From a moral standpoint, individuals have a right to psychological integrity and autonomy over their own minds. When memories cause severe, unrelenting moral distress that impairs a person\u2019s capacity to live ethically or function as a moral agent, allowing them to erase such memories respects their dignity and supports their ability to make future moral choices. Preventing access to this option imposes unnecessary suffering, which itself raises serious ethical concerns."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::2::465","side":"Con","angle":"Moral","text":"The Pro side\u2019s appeal to psychological autonomy ignores the moral value of retaining memories that reflect our actions and their consequences, even when they cause distress. Erasing such memories risks undermining personal responsibility and the moral growth that comes from confronting one\u2019s past. If individuals can excise memories of wrongdoing or harm, they may avoid the ethical reckoning necessary for genuine remorse and reform. This weakens the foundation of moral agency rather than supporting it."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Technological::3::466","side":"Pro","angle":"Technological","text":"Advances in neurotechnology now make targeted memory modification increasingly feasible, with techniques like optogenetics and CRISPR-based neural editing showing promise in selectively dampening or removing specific memory engrams in preclinical models. These technologies, while still emerging, could be refined to ensure precision and safety, minimizing unintended cognitive side effects. Regulated deployment of such tools would allow individuals to alleviate debilitating moral distress without compromising broader cognitive functions or identity coherence."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Technological::3::467","side":"Con","angle":"Technological","text":"The Pro side overstates the precision and readiness of current neurotechnologies, which remain experimental and prone to off-target effects that could disrupt unrelated memories or cognitive processes. Even if memory engrams can be identified, reliably isolating morally distressing content without altering associated beliefs, values, or identity remains scientifically unproven. Deploying such immature technology in clinical settings risks irreversible harm, especially when distressing memories are often interwoven with formative personal experiences. Without robust safeguards and reproducible accuracy, the technological basis for memory erasure is insufficient to justify its use."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::4::468","side":"Pro","angle":"Legal","text":"From a legal perspective, the right to erase morally distressing memories aligns with established principles of bodily and mental autonomy protected under human rights frameworks. Legal systems already recognize the right to refuse medical treatment and to control personal information; memory erasure can be viewed as an extension of these rights when it pertains to severe psychological harm. Regulated access through judicial or medical oversight would ensure that the intervention is used responsibly without undermining legal accountability for past actions."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::4::469","side":"Con","angle":"Legal","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that memory erasure aligns with legal autonomy ignores the fact that legal systems depend on consistent, unaltered memory for testimony, evidence, and personal accountability. Allowing individuals to erase memories could compromise the integrity of legal proceedings, especially in cases involving crimes or civil disputes where the individual\u2019s recollection is material. Even with judicial oversight, verifying the authenticity and completeness of a person\u2019s memory after potential erasure would be legally unworkable and undermine the evidentiary foundation of justice."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Political::5::470","side":"Pro","angle":"Political","text":"From a political standpoint, the state has a duty to protect individual autonomy and psychological well-being as core components of personal liberty. Allowing memory erasure in cases of severe moral distress aligns with liberal democratic principles that prioritize individual rights over paternalistic control, provided it is implemented within a robust regulatory framework. Such a policy would not absolve individuals of legal responsibilities but would recognize their right to mental self-determination, reinforcing the state\u2019s role in enabling citizens to lead dignified and functional lives."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Political::5::471","side":"Con","angle":"Political","text":"The Pro side\u2019s appeal to liberal democratic principles ignores the political risks of normalizing state-sanctioned memory alteration, which could be weaponized to suppress dissent or erase collective historical awareness. If governments gain the authority to approve or regulate which memories are deemed erasable, they may prioritize social stability over truth, undermining democratic accountability. Moreover, allowing individuals to opt out of morally distressing memories weakens the citizenry\u2019s capacity to engage critically with past injustices, which is essential for informed political participation and systemic reform."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::6::472","side":"Pro","angle":"Legal","text":"Legal frameworks can accommodate memory erasure without compromising judicial integrity by distinguishing between personal therapeutic use and evidentiary contexts. Courts already manage unreliable or incomplete testimony through cross-examination and corroborating evidence, and similar protocols could address concerns about altered memory. Regulated access to memory erasure, limited to cases of severe psychological harm and subject to independent oversight, would uphold individual rights while preserving the legal system\u2019s capacity to assign responsibility."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::6::473","side":"Con","angle":"Legal","text":"The previous Con turn overstates the threat to judicial integrity, as legal systems already function with imperfect and malleable human memory. Protocols for evaluating witness credibility and corroborating evidence can be adapted to account for therapeutic memory erasure, just as they handle memory distortion from trauma or time. Strict legal safeguards can ensure that memory modification does not interfere with ongoing investigations or obligations to testify truthfully to the best of one\u2019s recollection."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::7::474","side":"Pro","angle":"Economic","text":"The economic argument against memory erasure underestimates the long-term savings from preventing chronic mental health conditions that require decades of costly care. Even if initial development costs are high, economies of scale and technological maturation would likely reduce prices over time, similar to other medical innovations. Moreover, the productivity gains from a healthier, more functional workforce would offset public investment, especially when compared to the recurring costs of untreated trauma-related disability and social dependency."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::7::475","side":"Con","angle":"Economic","text":"The Pro side\u2019s economic optimism ignores the reality that high initial costs and limited scalability would likely restrict access to memory erasure, creating a two-tiered mental health system where only the affluent benefit. Public investment in such unproven technology would divert resources from cost-effective, widely accessible interventions like cognitive behavioral therapy and community mental health programs. Even with projected long-term savings, the opportunity cost of prioritizing speculative neurotechnology over established treatments represents a poor economic allocation."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::8::476","side":"Pro","angle":"Moral","text":"The moral permissibility of memory erasure hinges on respecting individual autonomy in the face of unbearable moral distress that impedes ethical functioning. While the Con side emphasizes moral growth through memory retention, it neglects cases where distress is so severe that it paralyzes moral agency rather than fostering it. Allowing erasure in such circumstances does not eliminate responsibility but enables individuals to engage meaningfully with future moral choices. The moral imperative to alleviate unnecessary suffering supports regulated access to this intervention."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::8::477","side":"Con","angle":"Moral","text":"The Con side\u2019s insistence on retaining morally distressing memories as essential for moral growth fails to account for situations where such memories incapacitate ethical reasoning rather than inform it. Severe moral distress can render individuals unable to act responsibly or empathetically, thereby undermining the very moral agency the Con seeks to protect. Permitting memory erasure in these cases does not evade responsibility but restores the capacity to engage meaningfully with moral obligations. The moral priority should be enabling functional moral agency, not preserving suffering for its own sake."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::9::478","side":"Pro","angle":"Social","text":"The social benefits of memory erasure extend beyond individual healing to broader community resilience. When people are freed from debilitating moral distress, they are more likely to engage constructively in social relationships, participate in community life, and contribute to collective well-being. This can reduce the intergenerational transmission of trauma and foster environments where empathy and cooperation are not overshadowed by unresolved pain. Regulated access to memory erasure thus supports a more inclusive and functional social fabric."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_004_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::9::479","side":"Con","angle":"Social","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that memory erasure strengthens social fabric overlooks how shared narratives of harm and accountability form the basis of trust and reconciliation in communities. If individuals can remove distressing memories, especially those involving wrongdoing, it may disrupt collective processes of healing that depend on acknowledgment and remorse. This selective forgetting could normalize avoidance over engagement, weakening the social norms that sustain mutual responsibility and long-term cohesion."}],"judges":{"GPT-5.2":[[0.0,0.88,0.0,0.52,0.0,0.25,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.2,0.0,0.58,0.03,0.0,0.0,0.9,0.05,0.0,0.0,0.15],[0.6,0.0,0.2,0.05,0.25,0.05,0.25,0.08,0.42,0.03,0.32,0.1,0.25,0.0,0.66,0.0,0.3,0.12,0.25,0.05],[0.0,0.78,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.72,0.0,0.3,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.45,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.86],[0.12,0.06,0.82,0.0,0.35,0.0,0.25,0.0,0.65,0.03,0.46,0.05,0.48,0.2,0.02,0.0,0.64,0.64,0.82,0.0],[0.0,0.6,0.0,0.76,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.66,0.0,0.66,0.0,0.76,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.18,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.6],[0.1,0.08,0.35,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.35,0.07,0.65,0.02,0.76,0.2,0.56,0.08,0.05,0.0,0.85,0.85,0.32,0.0],[0.0,0.66,0.0,0.75,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.79,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.6,0.03,0.02,0.0,0.78],[0.06,0.1,0.12,0.05,0.08,0.08,0.76,0.0,0.07,0.03,0.28,0.12,0.2,0.03,0.0,0.02,0.07,0.05,0.03,0.0],[0.02,0.65,0.0,0.7,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.56,0.0,0.9,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.15,0.0,0.02,0.0,0.79],[0.0,0.08,0.06,0.05,0.12,0.08,0.4,0.0,0.76,0.0,0.38,0.08,0.85,0.85,0.0,0.0,0.12,0.03,0.05,0.05],[0.0,0.66,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.47,0.03,0.0,0.0,0.66],[0.12,0.15,0.3,0.0,0.33,0.06,0.3,0.0,0.6,0.1,0.75,0.0,0.32,0.15,0.0,0.0,0.28,0.42,0.3,0.0],[0.0,0.64,0.0,0.66,0.0,0.65,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.92,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.33,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.56],[0.0,0.18,0.03,0.14,0.0,0.2,0.0,0.58,0.0,0.93,0.03,0.56,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.0,0.18],[0.0,0.